  [2m##[0m [1m[34mWhat I measured[0m

                           [2m │ [0;1mletibot[0m            [2m │ [0;1mleticl[0m
  [2m──────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┼───────[0m
  attach, 2000-item session[2m │ [0;1m1.6–2.7 s[0m of lexing[2m │ [0;1m0.29 s[0m

  Their commit [36m495d5d2[39m says the operator reported [3m"startup time skyrocketed. literally seconds"[0m after they migrated markdown to tree-sitter. Their own numbers: [1m133 ns/byte for block lexing, 250 ns/byte for[0m
  [1minline[0m, against Rust's ~3 ns/byte baseline — a stored 4–6 MB session is 1.6–2.7 s of parse.

  Their three levers, smallest-first, as they wrote them:

  1. [1mLex only what is drawn[0m — "bounds the work, changes the Block model".
  2. [1mCache the rendered history[0m — "strongest for [36m--continue[39m, needs a disk format".
  3. [1mPaint chrome first, fill over frames[0m — "touches what makes scrollback correct".

  [1mLever 1 is our architecture.[0m [36m%viewport-lines[39m walks the transcript [3mbackwards[0m and stops as soon as it has the lines the viewport needs, so cost is bounded by the screen and not the session — which is exactly
  why our 2000-item attach is 0.29 s and theirs is measured in seconds. I documented that measurement in S10 earlier; this is it paying off.

  So there's nothing to port. [1mThe optimization they need is the thing we happened to build.[0m

